  "Question": "Suppose that the number \\(a\\) satisfies the equation \\(4 = a + a^{-1}.\\) What is the value of \\(a^4 + a^{-4}?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(164\\)",
    "B": "\\(172\\)",
    "C": "\\(192\\)",
    "D": "\\(194\\)",
    "E": "\\(212\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Squaring the given sum produces the next even powers, with a simple middle term because \\(a\\cdot a^{-1}=1\\). The original equation requires \\(a\\ne0\\), so all reciprocal powers below are defined.\n\nUsing the perfect-square formula,\n\\[\n16=(a+a^{-1})^2=a^2+2+a^{-2},\n\\]\nso \\(a^2+a^{-2}=14\\). Squaring this new sum repeats the same useful pattern:\n\\[\n196=(a^2+a^{-2})^2=a^4+2+a^{-4},\n\\]\nsince \\(a^2a^{-2}=1\\). Therefore\n\\[\na^4+a^{-4}=196-2=\\boxed{194},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\text{D}\\). This calculation applies to every \\(a\\) satisfying the given equation.",
